  "notes": [
    "The point-read comparison favours Okto Grafx: the reference is asked for a row by primary key and pays a parse, a plan and an index probe, while the Okto Grafx side reads one row by RecordRef through a warm buffer pool. There is no query surface to measure yet (C10/C11 are later waves).",
    "The open-with-replay comparison is a LOWER BOUND on the Okto Grafx side: the recovery manager (C6) does not exist in the tree yet, so the measured work is WalManager.open plus a full checksum-verifying scan of the log, with nothing re-applied to the heap.",
    "Every number is a median of the kept samples with the full sample list beside it. The p95 and best-case multiples are reported too, because a ceiling met at the median and missed at the tail is a different fact.",
    "FR-15: the durable-commit multiple exceeds 10x, so the pipeline stops in the 'consult JP' state. Trading it for a fair lease would change D1 and is not a decision this harness may take.",
    "WHAT THE DURABLE-COMMIT NUMBER MEASURES: the 30 kept samples were taken against ONE database that is not reset between them, so kept sample k (0-based) pays for 5 + k commits already in the log -- the series spans 5 to 34 prior commits. It is visibly a rising curve, not a stable per-commit cost: the first kept sample is 161 ms and the last is 364 ms, and the mean of the second half (284 ms) is 1.34x the mean of the first (211 ms). The median recorded here therefore moves with the sample count. An independent reproduction reached 123.63x against this run's 109.40x with the same verdict, and a profile of it attributes the cost to a missing WAL LSN index (commit cost quadratic in commits already made) plus a pure-Python CRC-32C, with fsync at 0.2% and group commit measured at ~1.002x and rejected. The D5 ceiling stands unamended; this note states what the number is a reading of. Added 2026-08-22 without re-measuring: no value in this document was changed."
  ],
